@elimydlarz You are raising very valid points! Graphiti service could definitely helpful to the people who don't have a python service set up / don't really have a need for one outside of Graphiti.

This actually prompted an internal convo, we are in the process of making the graphiti service (the fast api server) better documented and more complete in terms of the methods it exposes from the graphiti package. Additionally, we will be removing the Zep specific terminology from graphiti service making way for more generic constructs from Graphiti to make the service independent. That applies to making group_id an optional field as well.

I think that the easiest option to run use Graphiti from deno environment, would be to start up Graphiti service as a docker image. Deno python runtime sounds promising as well, but I haven't got any experience with it, so can't really attest to how easy it would be to set up.

So I got this error and I managed to solve it by creating a proxy.

Example I did with google cloud functions or firebase functions:

```ts
export const azureOpenaiChatCompletionProxy:HttpsFunction = onRequest(
  {
    secrets: [azureOpenaiApiKey, azureOpenaiApiEndpoint],
  },
  async (request, response) => {
    try {
       const baseURL = `${azureOpenaiApiEndpoint.value()}/openai`;

      const client = createClient<paths>({ baseUrl: baseURL });

      logger.info("Request received");

      const { data, error } = await client.POST("/deployments/{deployment-id}/chat/completions", {
        params: {
          path: {
            "deployment-id": "YOUR_DEPLOYMENT_ID",
          },
          query: {
            "api-version": "2024-10-21",
          },
        },
        headers: {
          "api-key": azureOpenaiApiKey.value(),
        },
        body: request.body,
      });

      if (error) throw error;

      logger.info("Response processed");

      response.status(200).send(data);
    } catch (error) {
      logger.error("Azure OpenAI Proxy error occurred");

      response.status(500).json({ error: "An error occurred" });
    }
  });
```

I'm sure you can convert the above to python if need be and perhaps use aws, azure or gcloud to deploy and host it.

 I'm using it like this:

```py
azure_openai_client = AsyncOpenAI(
      base_url="https://your-proxy-endpoint.example.com/azureOpenAIProxy",
      api_key="N/A",
    )

    # Initialize Graphiti with Azure OpenAI clients
    graphiti = Graphiti(
        "bolt://localhost:7687",
        "neo4j_username",
        "your_secure_password",
        llm_client=OpenAIClient(
            client=azure_openai_client
        ),
        embedder=OpenAIEmbedder(
            config=OpenAIEmbedderConfig(
                base_url="https://your-proxy-endpoint.example.com/textEmbeddingProxy",
                api_key="N/A",
            ),
        ),
        # Optional: Configure the OpenAI cross encoder with Azure OpenAI
        cross_encoder=OpenAIRerankerClient(
            client=azure_openai_client
        )
    )
```

Aside for the rate limit issue, it works fine. I don't know what's being included in the request from graphiiti but the proxy seems to clear it up. 

I'm not sure why the `api_key` param is required when the `base_url` can point to a proxy that can inject it for you.

